analysis:
Both units are head-side test code in SSDPrefixCacheTests.swift, so there are no production behavior changes in the supplied lines. The patch excerpt shows only added tests (about lines 3140-3240) for SSDWholeRootMaintainer header-verdict caching. These tests check that a header corrupted after an earlier pass loses its ownership proof even with its mtime restored, and that a replaced lookalike is re-read. The tests reinforce the controls in T-041: files are owned only after a header check, TTL/LRU eviction applies only to owned files, and symlinks are not followed.

The visible tests cover:
- the production gate (DARKBLOOM_PREFIX_CACHE kill switch);
- the isolated test-root, which requires the ephemeral-key gate;
- the 30-minute TTL cap (env can only shorten);
- disk budget and write cap;
- HMAC-tagged filenames with no raw chain hash on disk;
- salt isolation;
- corrupt-block deletion;
- epoch retention on eviction, TTL sweep and corrupt-block removal (the T-041 detection_hint notes this, and the KEK wipe and per-file GCM are unchanged);
- binding-change rotation;
- symlink-replacement safety (outside files survive);
- undeletable-victim eviction.

Nothing in the tests weakens the TTL max, the HMAC naming or the scope folding. Test-only environment keys (DARKBLOOM_PREFIX_CACHE_ALLOW_EPHEMERAL, TEST_PERSISTENT_KEY, test root) are verified to be effective only together, but the production implementation of that gating is not in the supplied units. The production source for SSDWholeRootMaintainer and the verdict cache (which presumably uses ctime plus size and mtime) is not supplied. The next pass should confirm that the production change keys verdicts on ctime, inode and size, and does not skip header verification for changed files. Because of that missing source, I cannot confirm production safety.
